 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Composition of the two affine maps shows that commutativity is exactly $a_i(e^{v_i}-1)=b_i(e^{u_i}-1)$. Let $E(t)=(e^t-1)/t$ for $t\ne0$ and $E(0)=1$; its convergent series is $\sum_{n\ge0}t^n/(n+1)!$, so it is holomorphic and nonzero near zero. Since $a_i(o)\ne0$, the holomorphic function $\tau_i=(b_i/a_i)E(u_i)/E(v_i)$ is defined near $o$. The commutativity equation becomes exactly $v_i=\tau_i u_i$, and $\tau_i(o)=b_i(o)/a_i(o)$. The finitely many nonreal values permit one common neighborhood on which all $\operatorname{Im}\tau_i$ are nonzero. Thus all $\tau_i$ are nonzero, proving the equivalence of their zero logarithms and the asserted quotient limit. The argument is an identity of holomorphic functions on $Z$, so singular points cause no difficulty. [given, construct, algebra]
